# Closing Issues from a PR

GitHub closes an Issue when a closing verb (`close(s/d)`, `fix(es/ed)`, `resolve(s/d)`) stands directly before its number, even in a negated sentence.

- A PR that finishes an Issue puts `Closes #N` alone on a line under `## Related`.
- A PR that does not finish it never puts a closing verb before `#N`. Write `Part of #N` or `Refs #N`.
- An Issue whose subject the PR takes away (a check of code that will not be merged) is not finished by the PR: write `Refs #N`, and close the Issue as not planned after the merge. PR #427 first wrote a closing line for Issue #426.
- The line lives in the PR body only, never in a commit body.
- After a PR is created or its body edited, run `gh pr view <n> --json closingIssuesReferences` and compare the list with the intent.
- When the PR body itself says a condition of the Issue is not met, the PR does not close the Issue. Either move the unmet conditions to an Issue and a roadmap row of their own, or write `Part of #N`. Which of the two is the owner's to say when the owner set the conditions.

```text
# BAD: this closes #12
This does not close #12.

# GOOD
Part of #12. The NativeAOT check on #12 is still unmet.
```

Skill: `.agents/skills/github-closing/SKILL.md`.
